"header\", \"How can I install CUDA\")).get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n\nHere, the .filter() is necessary to ensure that we select the correct `article`. This is especially important in cases where the parent\nelement doesn't include relevant criteria for identifying a child element.\n</example>\n\n\nTips about this tree:\n - If you see a node as a child of an `iframe`, you must use `page.frame(name=...)`, and *then* access the node (via `.get_by_role` or similar).\n - Some elements will require interaction via `.select_option()`. They will be labeled as such.\n   This is because they are HTML `<select>` elements, and thus cannot be interacted with via clicks.\n\nYou also have this library of Python functions available to you. Think carefully about whether you think these can be used in your code. If you conclude that you can use a function, then simply call it. These functions are all available as global variables.\n<functions>\n# Skill: async def navigate_to_project_details(page, project_name: str)\nNavigates from the GitLab dashboard to the details page of a specified project.\n\nThis function assumes the user is on the GitLab dashboard page and clicks the link with the exact project name\nto view the project details. It uses exact matching for the project name to ensure accurate navigation.\n\nArgs:\n    page: The Playwright page object to interact with.\n    project_name (str): The name of the project to view details for. This should match the project name\n                      as displayed on the dashboard exactly (case-sensitive and full string match).\n\nBehavior:\n    - Starts by navigating to the root URL ('/') to ensure the correct starting state, typically the projects dashboard.\n    - Clicks the link with the exact project name provided, which navigates to the project details page.\n    - After execution, the page will display the details of the specified project.\n\nUsage Log:\n    - First usage: Successfully navigated to the details page for project 'Byte Blaze / My Unique Project' from the dashboard.\n      The page transitioned to the project details view without errors, confirming the project details were accessible.\n\nUnexpected Behavior:\n    - If the project name does not exist or is not visible on the dashboard, a Playwright TimeoutError may be raised.\n    - Using exact matching ensures that only the intended project is clicked, but if multiple projects have similar names,\n      this function may not handle ambiguity; ensure the project_name is unique and exact.\n\nSuggestions:\n    - Verify that the project name is correctly spelled and present on the dashboard before calling this function.\n    - If the dashboard structure changes, the selector may need adjustment, but the general approach of clicking the project link should remain valid.\n\n\n</functions>\n\nPlease think step by step and determine if further action needs to be taken. \nUse Playwright and complete the following Python function declaration:\n\nasync def act(page):\n    ...\n\nIf further action needs to be taken, provide a code snippet to interact with the page. \nPlease be noted that if you call an API that returns information retrieved from the website, you must return the result as-is, without any processing, filtering, or transformation. Do not attempt to interpret the data, extract values, or apply conditions. \nPlease return the API response results or print it out exactly as received. \n\n\nIf you have no more actions to perform, leave the `python_code` field blank and set the `terminate_with_result` field of your response to a non-empty string. Make sure that your terminate_with_result value is a direct response to the original task query.\n\nYour previous actions are:\n<previous_actions>\n<reasoning>\nI need to display the list of issues in the a11yproject/a11yproject.com repository that have labels related to help needed. I'm currently on the 'Projects \u00b7 GitLab' page. The accessibility tree shows a link for 'The A11Y Project / a11yproject.com' with an issues count of '41'. I should first navigate to that project's details page. The knowledge base has a function navigate_to_project_details that can do this. After navigating, I need to go to the issues page, filter for 'help needed' labels, and retrieve the list of issues. I must use accessibility tree-centric selectors like get_by_role and avoid CSS selectors. I'll plan: 1. Use navigate_to_project_details to go to the project page. 2. Click on the issues link (likely with name '41' or similar). 3. On the issues page, find a filter for labels and set it to 'help needed'. 4. Retrieve the list of issues using get_by_role for list items or similar. 5. Print the list exactly as received. I'll ensure to use exact matches where needed and handle potential UI variations.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    import asyncio\n    # Step 1: Navigate to the a11yproject/a11yproject.com project details page using the provided function\n    await navigate_to_project_details(page, \"The A11Y Project / a11yproject.com\")\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n    \n    # Step 2: Click on the issues link to go to the issues page\n    # From the accessibility tree, the issues link has name \"41\" (the count) and is a link\n    issues_link = page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"41\", exact=True)\n    await issues_link.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n    \n    # Step 3: Apply filter for labels related to 'help needed'\n    # Wait for the issues page to load and look for filter elements\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Allow UI to stabilize\n    \n    # Try to find a textbox to filter by label\n    filter_input = page.get_by_role(\"textbox\", name=\"Filter by label\")\n    if await filter_input.is_visible():\n        await filter_input.fill(\"help needed\")\n        await filter_input.press(\"Enter\")\n    else:\n        # Alternative: look for a button to open label filter dropdown\n        label_filter_button = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Label\")\n        if await label_filter_button.is_visible():\n            await label_filter_button.click()\n            await asyncio.sleep(1)\n            # Select 'help needed' from dropdown options\n            help_needed_option = page.get_by_role(\"option\", name=\"help needed\", exact=True)\n            await help_needed_option.click()\n        else:\n            # If no filter found, proceed without filtering\n            pass\n    \n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n    \n    # Step 4: Retrieve and display the list of issues\n    # Find issue elements using accessibility roles\n    # First, try to get list items that represent issues\n    issue_elements = page.get_by_role(\"listitem\")\n    count = await issue_elements.count()\n    if count == 0:\n        # Fallback: look for articles or other containers\n        issue_elements = page.get_by_role(\"article\")\n        count = await issue_elements.count()\n    \n    issues_list = []\n    for i in range(count):\n        element = issue_elements.nth(i)\n        issue_text = await element.text_content()\n        issues_list.append(issue_text)\n    \n    # Display the list by printing it exactly as retrieved\n    for issue in issues_list:\n        print(issue)\n    \n    # Since the task is to display the list, we can terminate after printing\n    return issues_list\n</code>\n\n<stdout>\n  \n        Switch to\n          \n      Projects\n          \n      Groups\n          \n      Your work\n          \n      Explore\n           Frequently visited  \n      Projects you visit often will appear here\n         \n      View all projects\n      \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n/\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn this project\n\nNew issue\nNew merge request\nNew snippet\n\n  Invite members\n  \ud83e\udd1d\n\n\n\nIn GitLab\n\nNew project/repository\nNew group\nNew snippet\n\n\n\n\n13\n\n\n0\n\n\n\n\nMerge requests\n\n\nAssigned\n0\n\n\nReview requests